            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Firstfruits of the harvest symbolizing the resurrection of Christ, as well as humility, poverty, and God's provision for the lowly.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Leviticus 23:10-11\",\"1 Corinthians 15:20\",\"Ruth 1:22\",\"John 6:9\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Poverty, lowliness, and human weakness (often referred to as the bread of the poor).\",\"basis\":[\"Judges 7:13\",\"Ezekiel 4:12\",\"John 6:9\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Economic hardship, famine, or scarcity.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 6:6\",\"2 Kings 7:1\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"Suspicion of sin, judgment, or jealousy.\",\"basis\":[\"Numbers 5:15\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"In biblical times, barley was cheaper and considered inferior to wheat, making it the staple of the poor. Christian interpretation heavily focuses on the timing of the barley harvest during Passover, linking the wave sheaf of barley to Christ's resurrection. Additionally, God's use of barley (like Gideon's barley loaf or Jesus multiplying barley loaves) symbolizes God using the weak and humble to accomplish His purposes.\"}",
